      "nodes": [
        {
          "id": "az104-d4-n01",
          "title": "Virtual networks and subnets",
          "concept_ids": [
            "azure.network.vnet_subnet"
          ],
          "prerequisite_node_ids": [],
          "map_position": {
            "x": 0.12,
            "y": 0.1
          },
          "prompts": [
            {
              "id": "az104-d4-n01-what",
              "kind": "what",
              "label": "WHAT IT IS",
              "placeholder": "",
              "required": true,
              "reveal": {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "An Azure virtual network (VNet) is a private IP network boundary in Azure. Subnets divide the VNet address space into smaller segments where resources are placed and policies/routes can be associated."
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "az104-d4-n01-why",
              "kind": "when",
              "label": "WHY IT MATTERS",
              "placeholder": "",
              "required": true,
              "reveal": {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Good subnet design creates clear network boundaries without requiring a separate VNet for every small workload component."
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "az104-d4-n01-model",
              "kind": "mental_model",
              "label": "HOW TO THINK ABOUT IT",
              "placeholder": "",
              "required": true,
              "reveal": {
                "type": "sequence",
                "items": [
                  "Choose a non-overlapping private address space",
                  "Create subnets with sufficient address capacity",
                  "Place resources in the appropriate subnet",
                  "Associate security/routing features where needed",
                  "Verify connectivity and DNS behavior"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "az104-d4-n01-clue",
              "kind": "exam_clue",
              "label": "EXAM / PRACTICAL CLUE",
              "placeholder": "",
              "required": true,
              "reveal": {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "When the question asks where an NSG or route table should affect a group of resources, the subnet is often the relevant network boundary."
              }
            }
          ],
          "source_refs": [
            {
              "title": "Study guide for Exam AZ-104: Microsoft Azure Administrator",
              "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/credentials/certifications/resources/study-guides/az-104"
            },
            {
              "title": "Virtual networks and virtual machines in Azure",
              "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/network-overview"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "az104-d4-n02",
          "title": "Virtual network peering",
          "concept_ids": [
            "azure.network.peering"
          ],
          "prerequisite_node_ids": [
            "az104-d4-n01"
          ],
          "map_position": {
            "x": 0.31,
            "y": 0.1
          },
          "prompts": [
            {
              "id": "az104-d4-n02-what",
              "kind": "what",
              "label": "WHAT IT IS",
              "placeholder": "",
              "required": true,
              "reveal": {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "VNet peering connects two Azure virtual networks so resources can communicate privately using the Microsoft backbone. Each VNet keeps its own address space and management boundary."
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "az104-d4-n02-why",
              "kind": "when",
              "label": "WHY IT MATTERS",
              "placeholder": "",
              "required": true,
              "reveal": {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Peering is a direct network connection, not a merge of the two VNets."
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "az104-d4-n02-model",
              "kind": "mental_model",
              "label": "HOW TO THINK ABOUT IT",
              "placeholder": "",
              "required": true,
              "reveal": {
                "type": "bullets",
                "items": [
                  "Peered address spaces must not overlap.",
                  "Peering is not transitive by default: VNet A↔B and B↔C does not automatically provide A↔C connectivity.",
                  "Routing/security settings still matter after peering is created."
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "az104-d4-n02-clue",
              "kind": "exam_clue",
              "label": "EXAM / PRACTICAL CLUE",
              "placeholder": "",
              "required": true,
              "reveal": {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "“Connect two non-overlapping VNets privately” points to peering. If A must reach C through B, design explicit routing/connectivity instead of assuming transitivity."
              }
            }
          ],
          "source_refs": [
            {
              "title": "Study guide for Exam AZ-104: Microsoft Azure Administrator",
              "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/credentials/certifications/resources/study-guides/az-104"
            },
            {
              "title": "Virtual networks and virtual machines in Azure",
              "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/network-overview"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "az104-d4-n03",
          "title": "Public IP addresses",
          "concept_ids": [
            "azure.network.public_ip"
          ],
          "prerequisite_node_ids": [],
          "map_position": {
            "x": 0.5,
            "y": 0.1
          },
          "prompts": [
            {
              "id": "az104-d4-n03-what",
              "kind": "what",
              "label": "WHAT IT IS",
              "placeholder": "",
              "required": true,
              "reveal": {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "An Azure public IP address resource provides Internet-routable addressing that can be associated with supported Azure resources or front ends."
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "az104-d4-n03-why",
              "kind": "when",
              "label": "WHY IT MATTERS",
              "placeholder": "",
              "required": true,
              "reveal": {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Use a public IP only when a resource or service front end needs direct Internet addressing; private-only designs should avoid unnecessary public exposure."
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "az104-d4-n03-model",
              "kind": "mental_model",
              "label": "HOW TO THINK ABOUT IT",
              "placeholder": "",
              "required": true,
              "reveal": {
                "type": "comparison",
                "columns": [
                  {
                    "title": "Public IP",
                    "items": [
                      "Internet-routable address",
                      "Used by supported resources/front ends such as public load balancers",
                      "Adds an Internet-facing surface to protect"
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "title": "Private IP",
                    "items": [
                      "Reachable within connected private networks",
                      "Assigned in a VNet/subnet context",
                      "Used for internal communication"
                    ]
                  }
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "az104-d4-n03-clue",
              "kind": "exam_clue",
              "label": "EXAM / PRACTICAL CLUE",
              "placeholder": "",
              "required": true,
              "reveal": {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "A public IP is an address resource, not a firewall rule. An NSG or service firewall still determines whether traffic is allowed."
              }
            }
          ],
          "source_refs": [
            {
              "title": "Study guide for Exam AZ-104: Microsoft Azure Administrator",
              "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/credentials/certifications/resources/study-guides/az-104"
            },
            {
              "title": "Virtual networks and virtual machines in Azure",
              "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/network-overview"
